## Polynomial Contrasts

If your groups have some natural *ordering* and *equal intervals* between levels, you may also be interested in testing a polynomial contrast. 

Polynomial contrasts tests for polynomial relationships in the outcome. If you have $J$ groups, you can test for $J-1$ polynomial contrasts, e.g.,

:::: {.columns}
::: {.column width="33%"}
**Linear** ($J \geq2$)

$y = \beta_0 + \beta_1x$


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](lab_09_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-26-1.svg){width=192}
:::
:::


:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
**Quadratic** ($J \geq 3$)

$y = \beta_0 + \beta_1x + \beta_2x^2$


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](lab_09_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-27-1.svg){width=192}
:::
:::



:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
**Cubic** ($J \geq 4$)

$y = \beta_0 + \beta_1x + \beta_2x^2 + \beta_3x^3$


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![](lab_09_files/figure-revealjs/unnamed-chunk-28-1.svg){width=192}
:::
:::

:::
::::


